      "message": "We can add two small static inline functions at the top of `rollback.c` to completely abstract the `1` and `0` and make the call sites read like plain English:\n\n```c\nstatic inline void acquire_software_flash_lock(void) {\n#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_EXTERNAL_STORAGE)\n\tcrec_flash_lock_mapped_storage(1);\n#endif\n}\n\nstatic inline void release_software_flash_lock(void) {\n#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_EXTERNAL_STORAGE)\n\tcrec_flash_lock_mapped_storage(0);\n#endif\n}\n\n```\n\nThen, inside `unlock_rollback()`, you simply call `acquire_software_flash_lock();`. This entirely removes the need for `#ifdef` logic scattered throughout the functions and isolates the \"inverted\" concept.",
